{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and one of the most influential nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. He was born in 1140 the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II which gave him a strong claim to the throne of Jerusalem. Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. His early years as count were marked by regency and political instability as powerful nobles vied for control. His mother Hodierna and his uncle Joscelin II of Edessa played key roles in guiding his early rule. Raymond was eventually able to assert his authority and became a capable and respected leader.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became deeply involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a frequent rival of the king and often clashed with other nobles over power and influence. He was a staunch supporter of the Knights Templar and maintained close ties with the military orders. In 1174 he was appointed regent of Jerusalem after the death of King Amalric I and the ascension of the young Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond served as regent for several years and was instrumental in managing the kingdom during a time of great threat from Saladin. He led military campaigns and negotiated alliances but his leadership was often questioned due to his cautious nature and perceived indecisiveness.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III faced increasing criticism as the Kingdom of Jerusalem deteriorated. He was accused of being too hesitant to confront Saladin and of prioritizing his own interests over those of the kingdom. In 1182 he was removed from the regency but remained a powerful figure. He was one of the few nobles who advocated for peace with Saladin and was even suspected of secret negotiations. His reputation suffered further after the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187. Raymond was present at the battle and survived but the kingdom collapsed soon after. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Tripoli to Muslim forces. His death marked the end of an era of noble dominance in the Crusader states. Raymond III left behind a complex legacy as both a defender of Christendom and a controversial political player whose caution may have hastened the decline of the Crusader kingdoms.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0007267932523973286}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025102324434556067}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.802703940891661e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -1.1368882656097412}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -4.076874756719917e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and"